      "content": "Yes, AWS organizations can have Service Control Policies (SCPs) that enforce permissions across all accounts within the organization, which might override some permissions granted by IAM roles if not carefully managed. Here's what you can check regarding SCPs:\n\n1. Verify if there are any SCPs attached to your AWS Organization or any Organizational Unit (OU) that contains your AWS account. SCPs may have broader or more restrictive permissions than your IAM roles.\n\n2. Review the SCPs in your account to ensure they do not have permissions denied rules that would affect the IAM actions you need, specifically the `s3:PutObject` action.\n\n3. If you find SCPs affecting your IAM policies, you may need to request changes from your AWS organization admins to adjust the policy restrictions.\n\n4. Ensure that your Lambda execution role permission isn't inadvertently being overridden by higher-level permissions at the organization level.\n\nIt's crucial to have a team or a breakout discussion with your AWS organization admins about permissions when SCPs are in play, to coordinate and ensure everyone is aware of the policies and potential impacts on permissions and security.",
